  "body": "## Finding description and impact\n\nThe `TrailsIntentEntrypoint` contract computes its EIP-712 `DOMAIN_SEPARATOR` once in the constructor and stores it as an immutable value:\n\n```solidity\n    constructor() {\n        DOMAIN_SEPARATOR = keccak256(\n            abi.encode(\n                keccak256(\"EIP712Domain(string name,string version,uint256 chainId,address verifyingContract)\"),\n                keccak256(bytes(\"TrailsIntentEntrypoint\")),\n                keccak256(bytes(VERSION)),\n                block.chainid,\n                address(this)\n            )\n        );\n    }\n```\nBecause `block.chainid` is read once at deployment, the stored `DOMAIN_SEPARATOR` permanently embeds the original chain ID.\nIf the chain later forks or the chain ID changes, this cached domain separator becomes outdated. As a result, the contract will continue validating signatures against an incorrect chain ID.\n\nThis causes all off-chain signatures to become invalid on the new chain, breaking signature-based flows such as `depositToIntent` and `depositToIntentWithPermit`.\n\nAny signature generated on the new chain will not match the original domain separator. This creates a permanent denial-of-service for intent verification on the forked chain.\n## Recommended mitigation steps\nCalculate `DOMAIN_SEPARATOR` dynamically in a getter function.",
  "proof_of_concept": "Add this PoC in path: `test/PoC.t.sol`:\n\nThis PoC demonstrates how the interaction fails due to the signature being invalid on the new chain because the `block.chainid` changed after a fork.\n\nRun with `forge test --match-test testIntentSignatureBreaksAfterChainIdChange`\n\nPoC:\n```solidity\n// SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT\npragma solidity ^0.8.30;\n\nimport \"forge-std/Test.sol\";\nimport \"../src/TrailsIntentEntrypoint.sol\";\nimport \"@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/ERC20.sol\";\nimport \"@openzeppelin/contracts/token/ERC20/extensions/ERC20Permit.sol\";\n\ncontract MockERC20Permit is ERC20, ERC20Permit {\n    constructor() ERC20(\"Mock Token\", \"MTK\") ERC20Permit(\"Mock Token\") {\n        _mint(msg.sender, 1_000_000 * 10 ** decimals());\n    }\n}\n\ncontract DomainSeparatorPoC is Test {\n    TrailsIntentEntrypoint public entrypoint;\n    MockERC20Permit public token;\n\n    address public user;\n    uint256 public userKey;\n\n    function setUp() public {\n        // standard deterministic key/address for tests\n        userKey = 0x123456789abcdef123456789abcdef123456789abcdef123456789abcdef1234;\n        user = vm.addr(uint256(userKey));\n\n        // Deploy contracts\n        entrypoint = new TrailsIntentEntrypoint();\n        token = new MockERC20Permit();\n\n        // Fund user\n        token.transfer(user, 1_000_000 * 10 ** token.decimals());\n    }\n\n    /// @notice Demonstrates that an intent signature created under chainId A fails after chainId is changed to B\n    function testIntentSignatureBreaksAfterChainIdChange() public {\n        // prepare parameters\n        address intentAddress = address(0xBEEF);\n        uint256 amount = 100 * 10 ** token.decimals();\n        uint256 feeAmount = 0;\n        address feeCollector = address(0);\n        uint256 deadline = block.timestamp + 1 hours;\n        uint256 nonce = entrypoint.nonces(user);\n\n        // -----------------------\n        // Create intent signature under current chainId (call this chain A)\n        // -----------------------\n        // Build struct hash exactly as contract does (note contract uses chainid() in assembly)\n        bytes32 structHash = keccak256(\n            abi.encode(\n                entrypoint.TRAILS_INTENT_TYPEHASH(),\n                user,\n                address(token),\n                amount,\n                intentAddress,\n                deadline,\n                block.chainid,\n                nonce,\n                feeAmount,\n                feeCollector\n            )\n        );\n\n        // Build digest using the contract's stored DOMAIN_SEPARATOR (calculated at deployment)\n        bytes32 domainSeparatorAtDeploy = entrypoint.DOMAIN_SEPARATOR();\n        bytes32 digest = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\"\\x19\\x01\", domainSeparatorAtDeploy, structHash));\n\n        (uint8 v, bytes32 r, bytes32 s) = vm.sign(uint256(userKey), digest);\n\n        // Allow user to be msg.sender for token approvals and transfers as needed\n        vm.startPrank(user);\n\n        // Approve entrypoint for amount (for depositToIntent path) and for permit test\n        // Note: we'll use depositToIntentWithPermit which calls token.permit(...) first,\n        // so the permit must match expected permit flow. For PoC we only need to call\n        // depositToIntentWithPermit to hit signature verification.\n        // To simplify: we will use depositToIntent (no permit) because the core of the\n        // vulnerability is intent signature verification (DOMAIN_SEPARATOR).\n        vm.stopPrank();\n\n        // -----------------------\n        // Simulate a fork: change chainId to a new value (chain B)\n        // -----------------------\n        uint256 newChainId = block.chainid + 42; // arbitrary different chain id\n        vm.chainId(newChainId);\n\n        // Sanity: chain id changed in the EVM\n        assertEq(block.chainid, newChainId);\n\n        // -----------------------\n        // Now try to call depositToIntentWithPermit using the signature that was\n        // created under chain A's domain separator. It should revert because the\n        // contract still uses the old, cached DOMAIN_SEPARATOR.\n        // -----------------------\n\n        vm.startPrank(user);\n\n        // As depositToIntentWithPermit will attempt to call permit(...) on the ERC20Permit token,\n        // we need a permit signature as well. For demonstration, produce a permit signature\n        // using token.DOMAIN_SEPARATOR() at current (post-change) chain id. However, the core\n        // failure we're targeting is the intent signature (recovered != user) because entrypoint\n        // uses the cached DOMAIN_SEPARATOR from deployment.\n        //\n        // We'll craft a valid permit such that permit succeeds (signed after chain change).\n        uint256 permitAmount = amount;\n        bytes32 permitStructHash = keccak256(\n            abi.encode(\n                keccak256(\"Permit(address owner,address spender,uint256 value,uint256 nonce,uint256 deadline)\"),\n                user,\n                address(entrypoint),\n                permitAmount,\n                token.nonces(user),\n                deadline\n            )\n        );\n\n        // Important: token.DOMAIN_SEPARATOR() is computed by OpenZeppelin's ERC20Permit and\n        // already uses token's domain (which includes token's own chainId at the time of signing).\n        // For robustness we compute the permit digest in the same way as OZ's permit implementation.\n        bytes32 tokenDomain = token.DOMAIN_SEPARATOR();\n        bytes32 permitDigest = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(\"\\x19\\x01\", tokenDomain, permitStructHash));\n        (uint8 pv, bytes32 pr, bytes32 ps) = vm.sign(uint256(userKey), permitDigest);\n\n        // Expect revert with InvalidIntentSignature (custom error) — the exact revert\n        // depends on how the contract uses ECDSA.recover; contract throws InvalidIntentSignature()\n        vm.expectRevert(abi.encodeWithSelector(TrailsIntentEntrypoint.InvalidIntentSignature.selector));\n        entrypoint.depositToIntentWithPermit(\n            user,\n            address(token),\n            amount,\n            permitAmount,\n            intentAddress,\n            deadline,\n            nonce,\n            feeAmount,\n            feeCollector,\n            pv,\n            pr,\n            ps,\n            v,\n            r,\n            s\n        );\n\n        vm.stopPrank();\n    }\n}\n\n```",
